play font-semibold tracking-tight text-base","children":"Example 3 — adding context to an ambiguous word"}]}],["$","div",null,{"ref":"$undefined","className":"p-6 pt-0 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Translating “I will go to the bank” alone is unclear, because the engine cannot tell a financial bank from a river bank. Rewrite it as “I will go to the bank to withdraw money” and the Hindi output will use the word for a financial bank. A few extra words of context often fix an odd result."}]]}],["$","div","Example 4 — choosing the right level of respect",{"ref":"$undefined","className":"rounded-2xl border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-[var(--v2-shadow-sm)]","children":[["$","div",null,{"ref":"$undefined","className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6 pb-2","children":["$","h3",null,{"ref":"$undefined","className":"font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-base","children":"Example 4 — choosing the right level of respect"}]}],["$","div",null,{"ref":"$undefined","className":"p-6 pt-0 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"You want to ask a senior colleague, “Can you check this file?”. If the Hindi result uses the casual “तुम”, change it to “आप” before sending it. The meaning is the same, but the tone is much more appropriate in an office setting."}]]}]]}]]}]
